      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "To determine the least whole number of hours that is greater than \\(4\\) hours and is \"clock equivalent\" to its square number of hours, we must find a number \\( n \\) such that \\( n \\equiv n^2 \\pmod{12} \\).\n\nFirst, let's systematically explore possible values of \\( n \\):\n\n- For \\( n = 5 \\):\n  \\[\n  5^2 = 25\n  \\]\n  Since we are working on a 12-hour clock, we reduce 25 modulo 12:\n  \\[\n  25 \\mod 12 = 1\n  \\]\n  Thus, \\( 5 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{12} \\).\n\n- For \\( n = 6 \\):\n  \\[\n  6^2 = 36\n  \\]\n  Reducing 36 modulo 12:\n  \\[\n  36 \\mod 12 = 0\n  \\]\n  Therefore, \\( 6 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{12} \\).\n\n- For \\( n = 7 \\):\n  \\[\n  7^2 = 49\n  \\]\n  Reducing 49 modulo 12:\n  \\[\n  49 \\mod 12 = 1\n  \\]\n  Thus, \\( 7 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{12} \\).\n\n- For \\( n = 8 \\):\n  \\[\n  8^2 = 64\n  \\]\n  Reducing 64 modulo 12:\n  \\[\n  64 \\mod 12 = 4\n  \\]\n  Therefore, \\( 8 \\equiv 4 \\pmod{12} \\).\n\n- For \\( n = 9 \\):\n  \\[\n  9^2 = 81\n  \\]\n  Reducing 81 modulo 12:\n  \\[\n  81 \\mod 12 = 9\n  \\]\n  Therefore, \\( 9 \\equiv 9 \\pmod{12} \\).\n\n- For \\( n = 10 \\):\n  \\[\n  10^2 = 100\n  \\]\n  Reducing 100 modulo 12:\n  \\[\n  100 \\mod 12 = 4\n  \\]\n  Thus, \\( 10 \\equiv 4 \\pmod{12} \\).\n\n- For \\( n = 11 \\):\n  \\[\n  11^2 = 121\n  \\]\n  Reducing 121 modulo 12:\n  \\[\n  121 \\mod 12 = 1\n  \\]\n  Thus, \\( 11 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{12} \\).\n\n- For \\( n = 12 \\):\n  \\[\n  12^2 = 144\n  \\]\n  Reducing 144 modulo 12:\n  \\[\n  144 \\mod 12 = 0\n  \\]\n  Therefore, \\( 12 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{12} \\).\n\nAmong all the values we found, the only number less than \\( 12 \\) that satisfies the given condition is:\n\\[\n5\n\\]\n\nThus, the least whole number of hours greater than \\(4\\) which is \"clock equivalent\" to its square number of hours is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{7}\n\\]",
