    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To find the least whole number of hours greater than 4 that is clock equivalent to its square number,\n\n1. Let's denote the least whole number of hours as $x$. Thus $x^2 \\cong x$ (when considered on a $12$-hour clock).\n2. With that in mind, we set up our equation as follows:\n   $$x^2 \\equiv x \\pmod{12}$$\n3. Expanding the equation, we slice the equation into modulo equivalences:\n   $$x^2 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{12} \\implies x(x-1) \\equiv 0 \\pmod{12}$$\n4. Since the equality holds for both factors, we investigate each:\n   - Consider the case where $x \\equiv 0 \\pmod{12}$. Given $x \\in \\mathbb{Z}^*$, this seems impossible as multiplication by 12 will never yield back its initial number on the clock.\n   - Next, tackle $x-1 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{12}$. This implies that $x = 12k+1$ for any integer $k$.\n5. Now, we substitute $x = 12k+1$ into the equality:\n   $$x^2 \\equiv x \\pmod{12} \\implies (12k+1)^2 \\equiv 12k+1 \\pmod{12}$$\n6. Analyzing both sides modulo 12, this resolves to:\n   $$12k^2 + 24k + 1 \\equiv 12k + 1 \\pmod{12} \\implies 0 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{12}$$\n\nTherefore, the least whole number of hours greater than 4 that is \"clock equivalent\" to its square number is $13$. $\\boxed{13}$ hours.",
